          <h2 id="physiology">Understanding Women’s Physiology and Strength Development</h2>

          <h3>Muscle growth and individual differences</h3>
          <p>
            Women build muscle through the same biological processes as men. Absolute muscle size and strength are generally lower on average, but relative improvements from training are similar. Genetics, sleep, stress, training history, and nutrition create large differences between individuals of the same sex.
          </p>

          <h3>Estrogen and the menstrual cycle</h3>
          <p>
            Estrogen plays roles in muscle repair, bone maintenance, and tissue health. Research on the menstrual cycle and training, however, remains limited and inconsistent. The 2020 McNulty meta-analysis found only trivial average differences in performance across phases. Some women feel noticeably different at certain times of the month, and tracking your own patterns is reasonable. Cycle-based training is not required for everyone.
          </p>

          <h3>Age, perimenopause, and menopause</h3>
          <p>
            Muscle mass and strength decline gradually with age unless countered by training. Around perimenopause and menopause, falling estrogen is associated with accelerated bone loss and changes in body composition. Resistance training is one of the most useful tools to counter these changes.
          </p>

          <h3>Recovery and energy availability</h3>
          <p>
            Training adaptations occur during recovery. Chronically eating too little for your activity level, known as low energy availability, can impair recovery, bone health, menstrual function, and performance. If your periods become irregular or stop while you are training, speak with a healthcare provider.
          </p>

          <h2 id="best-exercises">Best Strength Training Exercises for Women</h2>
