          <h2 id="how-to-start">How to Start Strength Training: A Beginner’s Guide</h2>
          <p>
            Starting out does not require complex gym routines. If you are brand new, our <a href={ROUTES.blog.path}>beginner workout guides</a> and step-by-step principles below will help you progress safely.
          </p>

          <h3>Choosing starting weights</h3>
          <p>
            Pick a load you can lift for 10–12 repetitions with good form while feeling that you could do roughly two to three more. If the last repetitions look sloppy, the weight is too heavy. If you feel nothing, it is too light.
          </p>

          <h3>Learning technique</h3>
          <p>
            Start with bodyweight or light loads. Film yourself, use a mirror, or work with a coach. Technique first, load second.
          </p>

          <h3>Frequency, sets, and repetitions</h3>
          <p>
            The American College of Sports Medicine (2009) recommends two to three nonconsecutive days per week for novices, with one to three sets of 8–12 repetitions for the major muscle groups. A 2016 meta-analysis by Schoenfeld and colleagues found that training each muscle at least twice weekly produced greater muscle growth than once weekly when volume was matched. A 2017 review found that both lighter and heavier loads can build muscle when sets are taken close to failure, so you do not need to lift maximally to benefit.
          </p>

          <h3>Warm-up and home training</h3>
          <p>
            Spend five to ten minutes on light movement, such as brisk walking, hip circles, and a few lighter practice sets of your first exercise. If you train at home, adjustable dumbbells, resistance bands, a bench or sturdy chair, and a floor space are enough to run a complete beginner program. Home workouts work best when you follow a written plan instead of improvising each day. If you enjoy interactive group coaching from home, consider joining our <a href={ROUTES.group.path}>online group fitness classes</a>.
          </p>

          <h3>Intensity, progression, and tracking</h3>
          <p>
            Use effort ratings: most sets should finish with one to three repetitions left in reserve. Once you complete all sets at the top of your rep range with good form, increase the weight slightly, usually 2.5–5%. Record exercises, weights, reps, and how the session felt.
          </p>

          <h2 id="weekly-plan">A Practical Weekly Strength Training Plan for Women</h2>
          <p>
            This five-day example splits training between lower and upper body. Each session lasts roughly 45–60 minutes, including a warm-up.
          </p>

          <DataTable
            caption="Sample 5-day upper/lower training schedule for intermediate lifters"
            headers={['Day', 'Training Focus', 'Example Exercises']}
            rows={[
              ['Monday', 'Lower body', 'Squats, lunges, glute bridges'],
              ['Tuesday', 'Upper body', 'Chest press, rows, shoulder press'],
              ['Wednesday', 'Recovery', 'Walking and mobility'],
              ['Thursday', 'Lower body', 'Romanian deadlifts, hip thrusts'],
              ['Friday', 'Upper body and core', 'Push-ups, pulldowns, planks'],
              ['Saturday', 'Optional activity', 'Light cardio or mobility'],
              ['Sunday', 'Rest', 'Recovery'],
            ]}
          />

          <p>
            <strong>For beginners:</strong> start with two or three full-body sessions per week (for example Monday, Wednesday, and Friday) using four to six exercises per session and two to three sets each. Move to the five-day split only when recovery feels comfortable.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Rest periods:</strong> about 60–90 seconds for lighter accessory lifts, and two to three minutes for heavy compound lifts such as squats and deadlifts. Progress when you can complete all sets and reps with good technique. Deload (reduce volume or load) for a week if fatigue builds, sleep suffers, or performance drops for several sessions.
          </p>

          <h2 id="nutrition">Nutrition for Women’s Strength Training</h2>
          <p>
            Nutrition requirements depend on body size, age, activity, goals, and health, so no single calorie or protein target fits everyone. For individualized meal planning, you can schedule a personalized <a href={ROUTES.contact.path}>nutrition consultation</a> with our coaching team.
          </p>

          <h3>Protein</h3>
          <p>
            The International Society of Sports Nutrition (2017) suggests 1.4–2.0 grams per kilogram of body weight per day for exercising individuals. A 2018 meta-analysis by Morton and colleagues found additional benefit from protein supplementation on muscle gain plateaued at around 1.6 g/kg/day, with a wide confidence range. For a 60 kg woman, that range equals about 84–120 grams daily. The Indian Council of Medical Research’s general recommendation (0.83 g/kg) is a minimum for the general population, not a target for people lifting weights. People with kidney disease or other conditions should consult their doctor before increasing protein.
          </p>
          <p>
            Spread protein across meals, roughly 20–40 grams per meal depending on size and appetite, rather than eating most of it at dinner.
          </p>

          <h3>Carbohydrates, fats, and hydration</h3>
          <p>
            Carbohydrates fuel training and replenish muscle glycogen. Rice, roti, oats, potatoes, and fruit are suitable sources. Dietary fats (nuts, seeds, ghee, mustard oil, olive oil, fish) support hormone production and absorption of fat-soluble vitamins. Aim to drink water regularly through the day and more in hot climates or long sessions, and let thirst and urine color guide you.
          </p>
          <p>
            Total energy intake matters as much as protein. If you are trying to lose fat, a modest deficit supports training better than aggressive dieting, which can compromise recovery and muscle retention. If your goal is muscle gain, eating at or slightly above maintenance helps most.
          </p>

          <h3>Key micronutrients</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Iron:</strong> needs are higher during menstruation, and iron deficiency is common among women in India. Do not supplement without testing. <strong>Calcium:</strong> adults generally need around 1,000 mg daily, rising to 1,200 mg for women over 50 (NIH). <strong>Vitamin D:</strong> deficiency is widespread even in sunny regions. Ask your doctor for a blood test before supplementing.
          </p>

          <h3>Pre- and post-workout meals</h3>
          <p>
            Eat a carbohydrate-rich meal or snack with some protein one to three hours before training, such as oats with curd and fruit, or poha with peanuts. Afterward, eat a protein-containing meal within a few hours, such as dal with rice and curd, paneer or egg bhurji with roti, or grilled chicken with vegetables. Explore our <a href={ROUTES.blog.path}>healthy nutrition guides</a> for practical daily recipes.
          </p>

